---
// La Forja, the product's shortest and most shareable URL. It hosts two
// things, in this order: an introduction that says what La Forja is and opens
// the way to the level map, and the free canvas (R1-D1) below it.
//
// The route does NOT move. Sending the playground to `/forja/playground` was
// proposed and rejected: it changes an existing route, which needs the owner's
// explicit approval, and it would break every link already shared. The intro
// is added ABOVE the canvas, never instead of it.
//
// `client:only="react"` per design D5, because the island measures the DOM (React
// Flow), so SSR output would be wasted work. This is the ONLY React island on
// the site: no other route pays for it, and the intro above is plain HTML.
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { ForjaCanvas } from '../../components/forja/canvas/ForjaCanvas';
import RankingStrip from '../../components/forja/RankingStrip.astro';
import { LEVELS } from '../../lib/forja/progression/levels';
import { isPlayable } from '../../lib/forja/progression/types';

// Every figure in the copy is counted from the content itself, never typed by
// hand: a hardcoded number goes stale the day somebody authors an exercise,
// and a landing page that overstates its own catalogue is the cheapest way to
// lose a reader who can count. Same filter the level map uses (EC6: only
// PILOT and PUBLISHED are ever served to a player).
const playableExercises = await getCollection('forjaExercises', ({ data }) => isPlayable(data.status));
const levelCount = LEVELS.length;
const exerciseCount = playableExercises.length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="La Forja: juego de arquitectura de software | Ingeniería sin filtros"
  description={`Diseñás un sistema en un lienzo y un motor te dice qué se rompe y por qué. ${levelCount} niveles, ${exerciseCount} ejercicios jugables.`}
  navVariant="blog"
  footerVariant="simple"
  noindex={true}
>
  <!--
    The playground is a tool, not an article, so it must not inherit the
    site's own centered `max-w-[1440px] mx-auto` shell (BaseLayout.astro,
    shared by every route and out of scope to edit here). `w-screen` plus
    `ml/mr-[calc(50%-50vw)]` is the standard "full-bleed breakout": it
    spans the true viewport width and re-centers itself under any
    ancestor, regardless of that ancestor's own max-width. BaseLayout's
    `overflow-x-hidden` on <body> (already present, unrelated to this
    page) absorbs any sub-pixel rounding, so this never causes a
    horizontal scrollbar. Full width minus a small gutter; the intro copy
    alone keeps a readable measure via its own `max-w-3xl`.
  -->
  <section class="relative ml-[calc(50%_-_50vw)] mr-[calc(50%_-_50vw)] w-screen px-4 py-8 md:px-6">
    <!--
      Two columns from `md` up, and deliberately so. The intro is what this
      route was missing, since it said nothing and linked nowhere, but it is
      paid for in vertical space, which is the playground's scarcest resource.
      Stacked, it pushed the canvas pane to 734px on a 1280-wide viewport
      (measured); side by side, with the copy at a wider measure, the pane
      starts at 593px. The call to action itself stays fully above the fold at
      1280x720 and at 390x844 (measured). That is the part that must never
      scroll out of reach.

      Default `justify-start`, never `justify-between`: the section is
      full-bleed, so space-between threw the button a thousand pixels away
      from the sentence that earns it.
    -->
    <header class="mb-7 flex flex-col gap-6 md:flex-row md:items-end md:gap-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-txt-primary">La Forja</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-txt-secondary">
          Diseñás el sistema en un lienzo, con componentes tipados, conexiones con contrato y zonas de
          confianza, y un motor te dice qué se rompe: qué regla violaste, qué parte del diagrama la
          dispara y qué consecuencia tiene en producción.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-txt-secondary">
          No hay una respuesta escondida: cualquier diseño que cumpla las garantías dentro del
          presupuesto llega a 100, tenga la forma que tenga, porque en producción tampoco existe una
          sola arquitectura correcta. Son <strong class="font-semibold text-txt-primary">{levelCount}
          niveles</strong> y <strong class="font-semibold text-txt-primary">{exerciseCount}
          ejercicios jugables</strong>, ordenados por dependencia: el nivel 4 puede discutir
          idempotencia porque el 3 ya definió qué es «la misma operación».
        </p>
      </div>

      <a
        href="/forja/niveles"
        class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-accent px-6 py-3 font-bold text-bg-deep transition-all duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgba(52,211,153,0.3)]"
      >
        Ver los {levelCount} niveles
      </a>
    </header>

    <div class="mb-4 max-w-3xl border-t border-border-subtle pt-4">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-base font-bold text-txt-primary">
        Lienzo libre, sin ejercicio y sin puntaje
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-txt-muted">
        Para probar una idea. El motor sólo puntúa dentro de un nivel, porque el puntaje se mide
        contra las garantías y el presupuesto que declara cada ejercicio.
        <span class="md:hidden">
          El editor necesita una pantalla de al menos 768 px y un puntero preciso. Desde este teléfono podés recorrer
          los niveles y leer las consignas completas.
        </span>
        <span class="hidden md:inline">
          Todo gesto tiene equivalente por teclado: <kbd>Tab</kbd> para moverte, <kbd>Enter</kbd> sobre una pieza de la
          biblioteca para crearla, <kbd>c</kbd> para empezar una conexión desde el componente enfocado y <kbd>Enter</kbd>
          sobre el destino para cerrarla, <kbd>Escape</kbd> para cancelarla, <kbd>Shift+F10</kbd> para abrir el menú de un
          componente o de una conexión, flechas para mover un componente seleccionado, <kbd>Delete</kbd> para borrar,
          <kbd>Ctrl+Z</kbd> para deshacer.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="hidden md:block">
      <ForjaCanvas client:only="react" requirePlayableWidth />
    </div>
    <!--
      RK1: a non-empty ranking element MUST be present in every La Forja
      view. The spec requires presence and non-zero size, not a specific
      position. Rendered here, outside the React island, so it is on the
      page regardless of which internal ForjaCanvas tab (Lienzo/Vista de
      lista/Resultado) is active. It never unmounts with them. Placed
      AFTER the canvas, not before: an earlier revision placed it above
      and pushed the canvas below a real 1280x720 viewport (measured, not
      guessed, since it broke an existing pointer-position test), which is
      exactly the kind of below-the-fold defect this whole change exists
      to fix, not reintroduce elsewhere.
    -->
    <RankingStrip />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
